Name: splitText

Type: function

Syntax: splitText(<string>, <regularExpression>)

Summary:
<return|Returns> an <array> of the pieces of a <string> that lie between
the matches of a <regular expression>.

Introduced: 0.9.16

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop, server

Example:
local tParts
put splitText("apples, pears;plums", "\s*[,;]\s*") into tParts
-- tParts[1] is "apples", tParts[2] is "pears", tParts[3] is "plums"

Example:
-- Split on any run of whitespace
put splitText(tLine, "\s+") into tWords

Example:
-- Split before each capital letter, keeping the letters
put splitText("oneTwoThree", "(?=[A-Z])") into tParts
-- tParts[1] is "one", tParts[2] is "Two", tParts[3] is "Three"

Parameters:
string (string):
The text to split.

regularExpression:
Any expression that evaluates to a regular expression. Each match of it
is a place where the <string> is cut.

Returns (array):
The <splitText> <function> returns a numerically keyed <array> of the
pieces of the <string>, in order. The text matched by the
<regularExpression> is not part of any piece. If the <string> is empty,
the <function> returns empty.

Description:
Use the <splitText> <function> to break a <string> into pieces where the
delimiter is a pattern rather than a fixed character, which is what the
<split> <command> requires.

If the <regularExpression> does not match anywhere, the <array> has one
element holding the whole <string>.

Two matches with nothing between them, or a match at the very start or
end of the <string>, give an empty piece: splitting "a,,b" on "," gives
"a", empty and "b".

A <regularExpression> that matches no characters, such as an empty
pattern or a lookahead, cuts the <string> between two characters without
removing anything. Such a match is ignored at the start and end of the
<string> and directly after another match, so it never produces an empty
piece. Splitting on an empty pattern gives the individual characters.

The search for each match begins where the previous match ended, but the
text before that point is still taken into account: "^" matches only at
the real start of the <string> (or of a line, with the "(?m)" option),
and "\b" and lookbehind assertions see the characters that precede the
match.

The <string> and <regularExpression> are always <case-sensitive>,
regardless of the setting of the <caseSensitive> <property>. (If you
need to make a <case-insensitive> comparison, use "(?i)" at the start of
the <regularExpression> to make the match case-insensitive.)

By default a <regular expression> works on text one UTF-16 code unit at
a time and treats only ASCII characters as letters and digits. Write
"(*UTF)" and "(*UCP)" at the very start of the <regularExpression> to
change this; see the <matchText> <function> for details.

If the <regularExpression> is not valid, the <function> throws an error
and a description of what is wrong with it, and where, is placed in
the result. The <function> also throws an error if matching needs so
many steps that it is abandoned (which can happen with nested repeats
such as "(a+)+$").

References: split (command), filter (command), matchAll (function),
matchText (function), replaceText (function),
array (glossary), regular expression (glossary),
case-sensitive (glossary), case-insensitive (glossary),
return (glossary), function (glossary), string (keyword),
caseSensitive (property)
